    <h2>Case Study: Eliminating 80% Bad Debt in "Benefits Verification"</h2>

    <h3>The Challenge</h3>
    <p>CVS Specialty Pharmacy (SPRx) manages complex, life-sustaining medical therapies. One of the highest-visibility, high-liability workflows is <strong>"Benefits Verification" (BV)</strong>. The BV representative is tasked with ensuring complex medications are covered by a patient's insurance plan, that prior authorizations are valid, and that estimated co-pays are transparently communicated to the patient.</p>

    <p>In the legacy system, representatives were confronted with an overwhelming wall of fragmented data without clear prioritization of the "best next action." This led to miscalculated copay estimators and frequent dispensing of medication before pre-certification or prior authorization was approved. <strong>For CVS, this operational breakdown was the direct source of 80% of all specialty pharmacy bad debt.</strong></p>

    <p>The design mandate: engineer an intelligent task framework that surfaces the immediate best next action, establishes guardrails against premature dispensing, protects patients from surprise medical bills, and prevents multi-million dollar business losses.</p>

    <!-- Legacy View -->
    <img src="/images/sprx/live_01.png" alt="Legacy view of a Major Medical task" class="project-image" />
    <p class="image-caption">Legacy view of a Major Medical (MAJ) task showing cognitive overload and absence of next-step clarity</p>

    <h3>Evaluating the Existing Task Friction</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Premature Focus on Outcomes:</strong> The legacy interface immediately demanded: "What action did you take today?" before guiding the representative through necessary validations.</li>
      <li><strong>Distracting Open Tasks:</strong> System alerts surfaced unrelated open patient tasks while omitting data required for the immediate case at hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Fragmented Multi-Screen Navigation:</strong> Representatives were forced to jump out of the core task into disconnected modules to cross-reference coverage.</li>
      <li><strong>Deceptive Status Trackers:</strong> Status indicators reflected the last approved Prior Authorization rather than the current active task state.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Analyzing the SOP &amp; Mapping Ways of Working</h3>
    <p>Partnering with Workforce Management, Product, and Specialty Operations, we diagrammed all potential clinical use cases across a 46-page Standard Operating Procedure (SOP). We prioritized steps by clinical risk and frequency, establishing an actionable decision hierarchy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What critical insurance information has already been verified and entered?</li>
      <li>Has the representative determined if a Prior Authorization (PA) is legally required?</li>
      <li>If required, what is the precise real-time status of that authorization?</li>
    </ul>

