    <h2>Case Study: Pharmacist PAl: Human-in-the-Loop AI Integration</h2>
    <p>Modernizing healthcare software requires pairing automation with human clinical oversight. I led the product design for <strong>Pharmacist PAl</strong>, an intelligent AI copilot assisting pharmacists in verifying prescriptions, checking complex dosages, and catching high-risk patient drug interactions.</p>

    <!-- Pharmacist PAl & AI Screen -->
    <img src="/images/sprx/live_12.png" alt="Pharmacist PAl AI interface" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <h3>Designing for Trust &amp; Explainability</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Explainable AI Confidence UX:</strong> Designed visual anchors that communicate <em>why</em> the model flagged a dosage anomaly, displaying the underlying clinical literature or patient history marker.</li>
      <li><strong>Dynamic Discrepancy Highlighting:</strong> Visual markers instantly isolate changes between new prescriptions and historical regimens, allowing pharmacists to quickly prepare patient consultations.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero-Latency Feedback Loops:</strong> Partnered with engineering to ensure real-time model inference without UI latency during high-volume verification shifts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Case Study: Uncovering "Over-Trust" in AI Production Verification</h2>
    <p>In high-stakes medical software, validating human behavioral response to AI is as critical as evaluating the model itself. When CVS explored integrating computer vision AI to assist pharmacists with pill counting and identifying co-mingled medications, I led a research team of two evaluators to test real-world pharmacist behavior.</p>

    <!-- Verification Interfaces & Findings -->
    <img src="/images/uxr/live_09.png" alt="Key Generative Insights from AI Verification" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <div class="image-grid-2">
      <img src="/images/uxr/live_10.png" alt="Production Verification Testing Setup" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/uxr/live_11.png" alt="Pharmacist sentiment and speed perception" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <h3>The Critical Finding: The "Automation Complacency" Trap</h3>
    <p>While initial pharmacist sentiment toward the AI was overwhelmingly positive, our evaluative testing uncovered a dangerous systemic risk: <strong>automation over-trust</strong>. When pharmacists believed the software was verifying the images for them, their scrutiny dropped significantly, leading them to overlook subtle co-mingled medication anomalies that they would have caught in manual workflows.</p>

    <p><strong>The Leadership Outcome:</strong> Based on our empirical findings, I advised executive leadership against an immediate nationwide rollout. Our research prevented a premature deployment that would have escalated liability, and we pivoted the product roadmap toward strict "two-stage confirmation" UX patterns that enforce active clinical review.</p>

    <h2>Leadership Retrospective</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>AI Trust Must Be Calibrated, Not Maximized:</strong> Making an AI system seem infallible causes users to turn off their own critical thinking. In high-liability healthcare environments, great design intentionally keeps the human clinician actively in the verification loop.</li>
      <li><strong>Complex Workflows Require Guided Next Steps:</strong> Analyzing a 46-page SOP showed us that representatives don't need all information at once; they need the single piece of verified data that unblocks their immediate next action.</li>
      <li><strong>Design Research Protects Both Patients and Margin:</strong> Identifying that 80% of bad debt stemmed from confusing UI states proved that fixing user experience directly protects bottom-line financial health.</li>
    </ol>
